---
# Static analysis of the subedit project. See docs/specs/00-fondations.md and
# docs/principes-de-conception.md.
#
# Project rule: every exclusion carries its justification in a comment.
# An unjustified exclusion is a defect, not a setting.
#
# The cppcoreguidelines families are selected one by one rather than enabled
# as a block: the block contains very noisy checks (pro-bounds-*,
# pro-type-vararg) that would drown out those that actually carry the
# memory-ownership rules.

Checks: >
  bugprone-*,
  performance-*,
  modernize-*,
  readability-*,
  misc-*,
  cppcoreguidelines-owning-memory,
  cppcoreguidelines-no-malloc,
  cppcoreguidelines-special-member-functions,
  cppcoreguidelines-prefer-member-initializer,
  cppcoreguidelines-pro-type-member-init,
  cppcoreguidelines-pro-type-const-cast,
  cppcoreguidelines-pro-type-reinterpret-cast,
  cppcoreguidelines-pro-type-static-cast-downcast,
  cppcoreguidelines-slicing,
  cppcoreguidelines-virtual-class-destructor,
  cppcoreguidelines-init-variables,
  cppcoreguidelines-narrowing-conversions,
  cppcoreguidelines-rvalue-reference-param-not-moved,
  cppcoreguidelines-misleading-capture-default-by-value,
  -modernize-use-trailing-return-type,
  -readability-identifier-length,
  -readability-braces-around-statements,
  -misc-no-recursion,
  -misc-include-cleaner,
  -bugprone-easily-swappable-parameters,
  -performance-enum-size,
  -readability-redundant-member-init,
  -readability-redundant-string-init

# Justification of the exclusions above:
#
# modernize-use-trailing-return-type
#   A style preference, not a defect. Would impose `auto f() -> int` everywhere.
# readability-identifier-length
#   A short name in a three-line scope is more readable than a long one.
# readability-braces-around-statements
#   clang-format already guarantees the indentation; the gain is nil and the
#   noise high.
# misc-no-recursion
#   Line breaking and tag parsing are naturally recursive.
# misc-include-cleaner
#   Many false positives with the Qt and standard library headers.
# bugprone-easily-swappable-parameters
#   Made obsolete by our strong-types rule: `Timestamp` and `Duration` not
#   being interchangeable, the compiler already covers the risk.
# performance-enum-size
#   Would require `enum class Format : std::uint8_t` on every enumeration of
#   the project, to save three bytes per value. That is speculative
#   optimisation, which docs/principes-de-conception.md forbids without a
#   measurement to back it. To be re-enabled if a profile ever shows it matters.
# readability-redundant-member-init
#   It calls "redundant" a `{}` that is not: it is the default member
#   initialiser, and it is what makes partial designated initialisation legal.
#   The core models are aggregates built by naming only the fields that
#   matter — `Subtitle{.start = t, .end = u}` — and removing the `{}` makes
#   `clang-diagnostic-missing-designated-field-initializers`, which is active
#   here, demand all the others.
#
#   Measured rather than assumed, in the review of #269: of fifteen `{}`
#   removed, three call sites stopped compiling. The two checks contradict
#   each other, and this one is wrong — the other says something true, that a
#   field forgotten in a designated initialisation is invisible.
# readability-redundant-string-init
#   Same defect as `readability-redundant-member-init`, for `std::string`
#   alone: `PatternCodeParts{std::string script = "";}` (#505) is what makes
#   `PatternCodeParts{.script = "Latn"}` legal elsewhere in the repository,
#   and removing it would have the same consequence — a missing field refused
#   by `clang-diagnostic-missing-designated-field-initializers`. A local
#   variable `std::string x = "";` remains a genuine redundancy; the check
#   has no such distinction, and nothing gives it one.

WarningsAsErrors: '*'
HeaderFilterRegex: 'src/.*\.hpp$'
FormatStyle: file

# NOLINT — the project's two local exemptions, and why they are such
#
# All the exclusions above apply to the whole project, which is the right grain
# for a check that is wanted nowhere. There are two cases where that grain is
# too coarse, and each carries its reason next to it:
#
#   core/time/frame_rate.hpp, `FrameRate::exactValueOf`
#     readability-magic-numbers. This is the table of the eight standard frame
#     rates: `24000/1001` is there the **definition** of
#     `StandardFrameRate::Fps23976`, not a number that came out of nowhere, and
#     the definition of a constant is the only place where a literal is allowed
#     to be bare.
#
#   src/test/gui/fake_video_player.hpp, `FakeVideoPlayer`
#     misc-non-private-member-variables-in-classes. The state retained by a
#     test double *is* its interface: a case reads `player.opened` and
#     `player.seeks` to say what the window did.
#
# Both checks therefore keep their full scope elsewhere.
#
# **A `.clang-tidy` could not have done it**, and this is measured rather than
# assumed: a configuration file applies to a directory, and for a diagnostic
# raised in a **header** it does not even apply to that one — it is the
# configuration of the translation unit that includes it that applies, and
# `frame_rate.hpp` is included from everywhere.
#
# A corollary worth knowing, discovered at the same time: `src/lib/subedit/gui/`
# and `src/test/` each have their own `.clang-tidy`, and they govern only the
# `.cpp` files of those directories. Their headers receive this one. Nobody
# could notice as long as `HeaderFilterRegex` caught no header — issue #269.

# **Nothing follows this list.** `src/scripts/verify-gates.sh` proves that the
# analysis key closes by adding an entry at the end of the file; text after it
# would make it inject its defect in the wrong place, and the proof would prove
# nothing any more. Same rule as the table of `docs/exigences.md`.

CheckOptions:
  # Naming conventions, see docs/specs/00-fondations.md.
  - key: readability-identifier-naming.NamespaceCase
    value: lower_case
  - key: readability-identifier-naming.ClassCase
    value: CamelCase
  - key: readability-identifier-naming.StructCase
    value: CamelCase
  - key: readability-identifier-naming.EnumCase
    value: CamelCase
  - key: readability-identifier-naming.EnumConstantCase
    value: CamelCase
  - key: readability-identifier-naming.TypeAliasCase
    value: CamelCase
  - key: readability-identifier-naming.TypedefCase
    value: CamelCase
  - key: readability-identifier-naming.FunctionCase
    value: camelBack
  - key: readability-identifier-naming.VariableCase
    value: camelBack
  - key: readability-identifier-naming.ParameterCase
    value: camelBack
  - key: readability-identifier-naming.PrivateMemberPrefix
    value: m_
  - key: readability-identifier-naming.ProtectedMemberPrefix
    value: m_
  - key: readability-identifier-naming.ConstexprVariableCase
    value: CamelCase
  - key: readability-identifier-naming.ConstexprVariablePrefix
    value: k
  - key: readability-identifier-naming.GlobalConstantCase
    value: CamelCase
  - key: readability-identifier-naming.GlobalConstantPrefix
    value: k
  - key: readability-identifier-naming.MacroDefinitionCase
    value: UPPER_CASE

  # The five names that `std::iterator_traits` imposes, and that are not renamed.
  #
  # (See also the "NOLINT" paragraph at the bottom of this file: the exemptions
  # that apply to the whole project are read here, the only one that applies to
  # a function is read there.)
  #
  # An iterator that calls its `value_type` something else is no longer an
  # iterator: the standard library reads them under these exact names, and the
  # project's case convention therefore does not concern them.
  # `Selection::Indices` carries them. The check saw nothing until now because
  # it looked at no header — see HeaderFilterRegex above.
  - key: readability-identifier-naming.TypeAliasIgnoredRegexp
    value: '(iterator_category|value_type|difference_type|reference|pointer)'

  # The core models are aggregates, and that is a decision.
  #
  # `Subtitle` carries its two positions and its two texts in the open,
  # `IndexRange` its two bounds: these are data, not objects guarding an
  # invariant. `docs/principes-de-conception.md` states it, and ADR 0008 goes
  # further for `Subtitle` — `end >= start` is **not** an invariant, since a
  # real file can carry the anomaly and the user must see it. Making the
  # members private only to expose them again through accessors would add a
  # layer that protects nothing.
  #
  # The option only disarms the check where **all** the members are public; a
  # class that hid some and not others is still flagged, which is indeed the
  # doubtful case.
  - key: misc-non-private-member-variables-in-classes.IgnoreClassesWithAllMemberVariablesBeingPublic
    value: true
